//! Entries slice -- Batch 177 (Plan 170 / Research 207) -- the EXL3/GB10
//! serving-recipe distill: 4 `kernel_opt` rules + the readout-trim scope
//! rider (`entries_gpu_26`), distilled from
//! `vcruz305/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-EXL3-DGX-Spark-recipe` @ `e0ebee8d`
//! (MIT; provenance three-deep: vcruz305/exllamav3 PR #2 -> turboderp-org/
//! exllamav3 -> QTIP arXiv:2406.11235, trellis-coded VQ). Recipe-class
//! source (72 KB README + 3 merged kernel patches) that grades above the
//! C+ recipe precedent because five components survived the dedup gate,
//! three carrying the author's own merged kernel diffs with quoted
//! before/after numbers. Executed AHEAD of the event-blocked B176 FWHT
//! rider (#29096 frozen through the 35th check; one-batch-per-day held,
//! B175 was 09-24). Every load-bearing quote re-verified verbatim at the
//! pin THIS batch (the repo moved +2 restructure commits past it; the
//! pin is load-bearing). All perf numbers are SOURCE-REPORTED (GB10
//! unified 128 GB aarch64, EXL3 3.05 bpw, 512-expert top-10 `MoE`) -- never
//! cite as ours (the B110/B123 precedent). The T2 re-verification caught
//! one verdict-table miss: `factor-affine-quant-scale-out-of-element-loop`
//! (`entries_gpu_01`) is the loop-stage half of candidate #2's fix -- the
//! verdict's phrase-grep (`scale.*factors out`, 0 hits) could not see a
//! rule whose ID is the mechanism; candidate #2 therefore lands as ONE
//! rule (the audit discipline) carrying the placement as its fix-regime
//! clause with an explicit kinship row, per the plan's T4 fork.
//! Batch 177 / Plan 170 / Research 207.

use crate::domains::kernel_opt::corpus::KernelRule;

/// Data table, not logic -- grows with distillation batches.
#[allow(clippy::too_many_lines)]
pub(super) fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ── N: (class, dtype) residency audit BEFORE pack-level bpw math;
        // the unquantized minority sets residency + round time ──────────
        // Source: patch 0001 (hc_mix.cu +256 ln, hyperconnections.py) +
        // tuning/mixaudit.py + mixq.py; the i8 A/B logs re-verified at the
        // pin (78.95 t/s @ 72.93% on vs 73.80 @ 73.12% off, code arm).
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "quantized-pack-residency-audit-unquantized-minority-first".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "Audit parameter bytes by (class, dtype) FIRST on any new quantized pack -- the UNQUANTIZED MINORITY sets residency and round time, not the headline bpw. Source-measured: the fp16 hyperconnection mixers in an otherwise 3-bit pack are 3.2 GB resident, 13.2 MB per site x 96 sites = 1.27 GB read per decode round, ~20% of the round at ~44% of spec bandwidth (each (M+1, R) block re-reads its weight row per stream); the headline bpw describes the majority and predicts NEITHER residency NOR round time. The audit tool's own header is the law (mixaudit.py, verbatim): 'How the 3.2 GB of fp16 GatedResidual weights in a 3-bit pack was found. Run this first on any new pack.' FIX REGIME (quantize the minority int8 + fp32 scales at load, choosing the scale granularity PER TENSOR so each scale is CONSTANT ALONG THAT TENSOR'S CONTRACTED DIM -- fn (M, H*D) contracted over H*D -> scale per row j; up (H, D/4, LR, 4) contracted over LR -> scale per channel (h, d) constant over LR): the scale then FACTORS OUT of the k-loop (sum_k s[k] * (q[k] * scale) == scale * sum_k s[k] * q[k], exact in real arithmetic), the fmaf chain and reduction order stay identical, and ONLY THE WEIGHT LOAD CHANGES (8-byte int8 loads = the same 8-wide step as the half kernel's 16-byte loads, half the bytes); the fp16 fused-path copies are freed (1.6 GB back) and the prefill path is untouched. THE PRECISION LADDER IS A MEASURED TWO-SIDED BOUND and the simulation runs BEFORE the kernel is written (mixq.py decides whether a kernel is worth writing): int8 sim acceptance 58-63% vs 63% fp16 (kept); int6 REJECTED for lane-aligned loads (the packing cannot keep the 8-wide step); int4 sim COLLAPSED acceptance to 20% (rejected). Measured end to end (GB10, cold greedy 400 tok, -mtp -ndt 5): 74.0 -> 79.1 / 55.5 -> 62.5 / 49.0 -> 53.0 t/s (code/devops/prose) at flat acceptance (73.1 -> 72.9 / 55.9 -> 59.2 / 46.1 -> 45.7); kernel parity vs fp32 torch on the DEQUANTIZED weights 1-2e-4 rel (mixed), ~1e-7 (post), R = 1..8; isolated per-call 1.14-1.34x. HONESTY CLAUSES: greedy trajectories diverge from fp16 at token 157/34/14 (code/devops/prose) -- parity is MEASURED, not bit-identity (the in-code note: 'in fp32 the rounding pattern differs slightly from multiplying inside the loop, but this is the SAME trajectory whether the reference is [the] fp16 kernel [on] dequantized weights or not'); and the ship posture is DEFAULT-OFF (EXL3_GR_INT8; the author's own 'pending a second box's numbers' -- inherit it). KINSHIP, compose: factor-affine-quant-scale-out-of-element-loop (entries_gpu_01) is the LOOP-STAGE exploit of an already-constant scale; THIS rule is the FORMAT-STAGE choice that CREATES the constant plus the audit that finds the target. Distinct from dequant-placement-by-component-traffic-share (which component dequantizes WHERE, per traffic share; this is which weight CLASS stays unquantized and the load-time fix), from outlier-span-escapes-quantization-via-lossless-sidecar (per-outlier escape INSIDE an otherwise-quantized tensor; this is whole-tensor minority classes), from hoist-group-invariant-scale-out-of-word-loop (register-caching a scale to cut redundant LOADS; no format choice). Source: vcruz305/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-EXL3-DGX-Spark-recipe @ e0ebee8ddc4391b5e66d86fdce993b0756e00986 (MIT) -- patch 0001 commit body + hc_mix.cu (the factors-out comment, the 8-byte-load note, the sj = 1.0f out-of-row escape) + hyperconnections.py (_quantize_int8 docstring: the granularity law verbatim); tuning/mixaudit.py + mixq.py; logs/i8_int8-{on,off}_dds_*.log. Batch 177 / Plan 170 / Research 207.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: the pack is described by its headline bpw; parameter bytes are\n// never audited by (class, dtype), so the fp16 minority class's residency\n// and per-round reads are invisible until the decode profile shows ~20% of\n// the round inside a '3-bit' model.\nfn pack_report_headline_bpw_only(total_params: u64, headline_bpw: f32) -> u64 {\n    (total_params as f32 * headline_bpw / 8.0) as u64\n    // predicts neither the 3.2 GB fp16 minority nor the 1.27 GB/round it\n    // re-reads (13.2 MB x 96 sites) -- the audit never ran\n}\n\nfn mixer_dot_fp16_minority(streams: &[f32], w_fp16: &[f32]) -> f32 {\n    let mut acc = 0.0f32;\n    for k in 0..w_fp16.len() {\n        acc = f32::mul_add(streams[k], w_fp16[k], acc); // half-width loads, 2x bytes\n    }\n    acc\n}".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: audit bytes by (class, dtype) FIRST (mixaudit: 'Run this first\n// on any new pack'); quantize the hot minority int8 with the scale chosen\n// CONSTANT ALONG THE CONTRACTED DIM so it factors out of the k-loop and only\n// the weight load changes -- and simulate the precision floor BEFORE writing\n// the kernel (mixq: int8 keeps acceptance 58-63% vs 63%; int4 collapses to 20%).\nfn pack_audit_bytes_by_class_dtype(sites: usize) -> (u64, u64) {\n    // fp16 mixers: 13.2 MB x 96 sites, re-read every round (~20% of it)\n    let fp16_minority_bytes = 13_200 * 1024 * sites;\n    // fix: int8 + per-row fp32 scale at load; fp16 fused copies freed (+1.6 GB)\n    let int8_minority_bytes = fp16_minority_bytes / 2;\n    (fp16_minority_bytes, int8_minority_bytes)\n}\n\nfn mixer_dot_int8_scale_outside_kloop(streams: &[f32], q: &[i8], scale: f32) -> f32 {\n    // scale constant along the contracted dim -> accumulate RAW, apply ONCE:\n    // sum_k s[k] * (q[k] * scale) == scale * sum_k s[k] * q[k]\n    let mut acc = 0.0f32;\n    for k in 0..q.len() {\n        acc = f32::mul_add(streams[k], q[k] as f32, acc); // SAME fmaf chain + order\n    }\n    acc * scale // applied ONCE outside the loop -- only the weight load changed\n}".to_string(),
        },

        // ── N: bpw changes buy MEMORY (context ceiling, residency,
        // acceptance), not decode, when decode is dequant-bound ─────────
        // Source: README "The 4.05 bpw revision" + docs/benchmark-data.json
        // (schema v2, context_curve 52.22 @3060 / 50.53 @24354; the
        // 3.05-vs-4.05 tables).
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "bpw-reduction-buys-memory-not-decode-when-dequant-bound".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "On trellis/codebook quantization formats the per-weight dequant cost barely grows with K, so decode is bound by DEQUANT COMPUTE rather than bytes moved -- and the usual 'lower bpw => faster decode' heuristic INVERTS: a bpw change buys MEMORY, not decode. Measured 3.05 vs 4.05 bpw, same build and harness: decode 52.22 -> 48.70 @4k, 50.53 -> 51.31 @32k, cold prefill 1,142 -> 1,137 -- ALL inside the sample spread (speed-neutral within noise BOTH directions). The cost is entirely memory-side and severe: +22 GiB resident (79 -> 100.81), --gpu-memory-utilization forced 0.80 -> 0.92, context ceiling HALVED 262,144 -> 131,072, MemAvailable 17-18 GiB -> 3.4 GiB. The ONE real gain is acceptance 68% -> 74% -- a higher-precision target agrees with its draft more often, which is a SPECULATION win, not a bandwidth one. The rule names WHICH axis a bpw change buys on (memory headroom + context ceiling, secondary acceptance; NOT decode speed) -- exactly the question an 'add format X' decision asks, and the warning an issue promising decode wins from a bpw advantage needs to read. REGIME: dequant-bound decode (per-weight dequant ~flat in K on codebook/trellis formats); ANTI-REGIME: bandwidth-bound decode, where lower bpw genuinely cuts bytes per cycle and the classic heuristic HOLDS -- decide which bound you are in before promising either direction (the same two-regime discipline as the corpus's other axis-naming rules; a single-direction claim is the defect). Source-reported (GB10 unified 128 GB, EXL3 3.05/4.05 bpw, Qwen3.8-Flash-Next 512-expert top-10) -- never cite as ours. Distinct from quantized-pack-residency-audit-unquantized-minority-first (same-batch kin: the AUDIT that finds a minority class INSIDE one pack; this is the FORMAT-LEVEL bpw axis decision across packs), from dequant-once-per-k-tile-into-shared-memory (a dequant PLACEMENT optimization inside the bound regime; this decides whether bpw moves the needle at all), from the bandwidth-bound upload/GEMV families (they live in the anti-regime). Source: README 'The 4.05 bpw revision' (the 3.05-vs-4.05 tables) + docs/benchmark-data.json (context_curve, hardware block, updated 2026-09-14, source_commit 943d662). Batch 177 / Plan 170 / Research 207.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: the pitch says lower bpw = fewer bytes moved = faster decode.\nfn bpw_decision_by_bandwidth_heuristic(bpw_low: f32, bpw_high: f32) -> (&'static str, f32) {\n    let bytes_ratio = bpw_low / bpw_high; // 3.05 / 4.05 = 0.75 -- '25% fewer bytes'\n    (\"decode_speedup_expected\", bytes_ratio)\n    // WRONG when decode is dequant-bound: measured 52.22 -> 48.70 @4k and\n    // 50.53 -> 51.31 @32k -- inside spread, both directions\n}".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: on codebook/trellis formats per-weight dequant cost is ~flat\n// in K, so decode is dequant-COMPUTE-bound; name the axis the bpw change\n// actually buys on -- memory + context ceiling, acceptance secondarily.\nfn bpw_decision_dequant_bound_regime() -> (&'static str, f64, f64, u32) {\n    // measured, same build + harness: decode inside spread; the real deltas:\n    (\"memory_headroom_and_context_ceiling\", 22.0, 74.0 - 68.0, 262_144 / 2)\n    // +22 GiB resident, acceptance 68 -> 74 (a speculation win, not\n    // bandwidth), context ceiling 262_144 -> 131_072 (HALVED) -- and\n    // ANTI-REGIME: bandwidth-bound decode keeps the classic heuristic\n}".to_string(),
        },

        // ── D: a host-gathered operand splits the CUDA graph, and the
        // PIECEWISE downgrade carries its own separable price ───────────
        // Source: README "The 4.05 bpw revision" (NVMe-table half) +
        // vllm-exl3 NGRAM_TABLE=disk; the resident-PIECEWISE cells
        // (48.25/48.01 vs 52.22/50.53 at k=3).
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "host-gather-splits-the-graph-and-the-split-is-the-separable-price".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "A HOST-GATHERED operand (a disk-backed / mmap'd table looked up per step) is a host synchronization point and CANNOT sit inside a full CUDA graph: disk mode runs PIECEWISE-only with the lookup as the splitting op. THE DECISION CONTENT is that the graph-mode downgrade is PRICED INDEPENDENTLY of the mechanism that forced it -- PIECEWISE ALONE measured 5-7% on the same model with the table still resident (48.25 / 48.01 against 52.22 / 50.53 at k=3), so the gather's own cost is the RESIDUAL, never the whole ~10%; an A/B that flips both the storage mode AND the graph mode attributes a separable constant to the mechanism under test. SECOND CLAUSE: rows FIRST-TOUCH page in from NVMe, which is why short-prompt samples start slow (32.6-46.0 tok/s across four) while the 32k figure lands where PIECEWISE alone would put it -- first-touch effects masquerade as per-prompt variance unless the working set is pre-touched before the cells are read. Net posture for the escape hatch: real, ~10% slower, +15 GiB headroom, and 262k context reachable where the resident table cannot reach it -- an explicit trade, priced by the separated cells. REGIME: an operand that must round-trip through host memory every step (disk-backed n-gram tables, host-mmap'd lookups); ANTI-REGIME: device-resident tables (full graphs, no split -- the default posture the escape hatch degrades FROM). Distinct from cuda-graph-scalar-via-device-pointer (keeping a graph FULL by removing one host-read scalar; this prices the downgrade when you CANNOT remove the gather), from batched-downloads-single-sync-cycle (collapsing N readbacks into one; the gather is ONE unavoidable per-step sync, not N), from no-blocking-sync-in-async-pipeline (a pipeline discipline; this is a graph-CAPTURE legality + attribution rule). Source: README 'The 4.05 bpw revision' (the NVMe table half: the PIECEWISE-only statement, the resident-PIECEWISE cells, the short-prompt first-touch figures, the +15 GiB / 262k posture) + vllm-exl3 NGRAM_TABLE=disk. Batch 177 / Plan 170 / Research 207.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: disk mode measures ~10% slower than resident, and the report\n// blames the host gather -- but PIECEWISE-only graph mode rides along with\n// the storage flip, so the A/B moved TWO variables.\nfn attribute_whole_delta_to_gather(resident_full: f64, disk_piecewise: f64) -> f64 {\n    resident_full / disk_piecewise - 1.0 // ~10% attributed wholly to the gather\n}".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: price the graph-mode downgrade ALONE (PIECEWISE with the\n// table still resident = 5-7%), then attribute only the residual to the\n// gather; pre-touch the working set so first-touch paging does not masquerade\n// as per-prompt variance.\nfn attribute_separably(\n    resident_full: f64,\n    resident_piecewise: f64, // the SAME model, table resident, forced PIECEWISE\n    disk_piecewise_pre_touched: f64,\n) -> (f64, f64) {\n    let mode_downgrade = resident_full / resident_piecewise - 1.0; // 5-7%: the separable price\n    let gather_residual = resident_piecewise / disk_piecewise_pre_touched - 1.0;\n    (mode_downgrade, gather_residual) // report BOTH; never fold them into one number\n}".to_string(),
        },

        // ── D: on unified-memory parts the host page cache IS
        // GPU-allocatable memory -- drop it deliberately, never blanketly ─
        // Source: tuning/drop-model-cache.sh (the fadvise sweep + its
        // comment) + tuning/README.md (the >=8 s workspace race; 9.5 vs
        // 2.5 min cold/warm load).
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "unified-memory-page-cache-is-device-allocatable".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "On unified-memory parts the HOST page cache competes with GPU ALLOCATION -- cached file pages count against GPU-allocatable RAM (drop-model-cache.sh, verbatim: 'unified memory: cached file pages count against GPU-allocatable RAM and exllamav3's autosplit refuses to load if mem_get_info() looks tight'), so without posix_fadvise(DONTNEED) on every pack file 'the autosplit loader refuses to load after any large file write', and back-to-back loads need >= 8 s apart 'or the second dies to a workspace race.' THE SAME CACHE IS ALSO THE SPEEDUP -- cold load 9.5 min from NVMe vs 2.5 min from page cache -- so the cache is a resource to DROP DELIBERATELY AT THE RIGHT MOMENT (after the pack is loaded, before the next large allocation), never to minimize or maximize blanketly: dropping it before the load pays 9.5 min; keeping it after the load starves the allocator. FIX SHAPE: a per-file fadvise(DONTNEED) sweep over the model dir (no root needed), issued between load-completion and the next large GPU allocation; keep it idempotent and per-directory so partial reloads re-drop only what they touched. The >= 8 s inter-load spacing is a SEPARATE hazard the drop does not cure -- record it beside the sweep, not inside it. REGIME: unified-memory parts (GB10-class) where host RAM == device RAM; ANTI-REGIME: discrete GPUs, where the host page cache is NOT device memory and the existing page-cache rules govern (B94 coalescing exploits the cache as a friend; B118 overlaps its cold page-ins; B123 gates read-ahead hints against read pools). Distinct from those three (they optimize ACCESS through the cache on discrete hosts; this accounts the cache itself as a contended device resource on unified parts), from hot-swap-weights-preserve-autotune-caches (load-path state preservation; this is load-path MEMORY accounting). Source: tuning/drop-model-cache.sh (the fadvise loop + the header comment) + tuning/README.md (the workspace-race + cold/warm cells). Batch 177 / Plan 170 / Research 207.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: the pack loads warm from page cache (2.5 min) and the cache is\n// left fully resident -- on a unified-memory part those pages ARE\n// GPU-allocatable RAM, so the next large allocation fails.\nfn load_pack_leave_cache_resident(model_dir: &str) -> bool {\n    let files = list_pack_files(model_dir);\n    read_all(&files); // warm: page cache now holds the whole pack\n    true // mem_get_info() now 'looks tight' -> the autosplit loader REFUSES\n         // the next load; back-to-back loads die to a workspace race anyway\n}\n\nfn list_pack_files(_dir: &str) -> Vec<std::path::PathBuf> { Vec::new() }\nfn read_all(_files: &[std::path::PathBuf]) {}".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: keep the cache FOR the load (2.5 min vs 9.5 min cold -- it is\n// the speedup, do not minimize blanketly), then drop it deliberately once\n// the pack is resident: a per-file fadvise(DONTNEED) sweep frees the\n// GPU-allocatable RAM the next allocation needs.\nfn load_then_drop_cache_deliberately(model_dir: &str) -> bool {\n    let files = list_pack_files(model_dir);\n    read_all(&files); // the cache IS the speedup -- keep it through the load\n    for f in &files {\n        drop_file_pages(f); // posix_fadvise(DONTNEED): free GPU-allocatable RAM\n    }\n    // the >= 8s inter-load spacing is a SEPARATE hazard -- the drop does not\n    // cure it; record it beside the sweep, not inside it\n    true\n}\n\nfn list_pack_files(_dir: &str) -> Vec<std::path::PathBuf> { Vec::new() }\nfn read_all(_files: &[std::path::PathBuf]) {}\nfn drop_file_pages(_f: &std::path::PathBuf) {} // posix_fadvise(fd, 0, 0, POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED)".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}
